Q: i need to show phonenumber hint on keyboard in ionic can any suggest for it. just like Instamart when i tried using which i shared in Body A: <p>It makes total sense why you're stuck here. You followed standard web development advice by using the <code>autocomplete=&quot;tel&quot;</code> attribute, but you aren't getting the result you want.</p>
<p>Here is the reality: <code>autocomplete=&quot;tel&quot;</code> is an HTML web standard. While it works well on standard mobile browsers (and perfectly on iOS Safari) to show a suggestion above the keyboard, it <strong>does not</strong> trigger the native Android &quot;Choose your phone number&quot; bottom sheet inside an Ionic Webview.</p>
<p>Apps like Instamart use native Android capabilities—specifically the <strong>Google Play Services Phone Number Hint API</strong>—to bring up that seamless bottom-sheet dialog. To get this exact native behavior in Ionic, you cannot rely purely on HTML tags; you have to bridge into native code using a Capacitor (or Cordova) plugin.</p>
<p>Here is how you can achieve the &quot;Instamart&quot; experience in your Ionic app:</p>
<h3>Step 1: Install a Native Plugin</h3>
<p>If you are using Capacitor (the modern standard for Ionic), you can use a community plugin designed exactly for this, such as <code>@ak3696/capacitor-phone-hint</code>.</p>
<pre><code>Run this in your terminal:

npm install @ak3696/capacitor-phone-hint
npx cap sync
</code></pre>
<h3>Step 2: Trigger the Hint in your Component</h3>
<p>Instead of relying on the keyboard to auto-suggest, you actively request the phone number when the user focuses on the input field or when the screen loads.</p>
<pre><code>import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { PhoneHint } from '@ak3696/capacitor-phone-hint';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-login',
  template: `
    &lt;ion-input 
      type=&quot;tel&quot; 
      [value]=&quot;phoneNumber&quot; 
      placeholder=&quot;Enter Mobile Number&quot; 
      (ionFocus)=&quot;getNativePhoneHint()&quot;&gt;
    &lt;/ion-input&gt;
  `
})
export class LoginPage {
  phoneNumber: string = '';

  async getNativePhoneHint() {
    try {
      // This fires the native Android bottom sheet!
      const result = await PhoneHint.requestHint(); 
      if (result &amp;&amp; result.phoneNumber) {
        this.phoneNumber = result.phoneNumber;
      }
    } catch (err) {
      // Fails silently if the user dismisses the dialog or no number is found
      console.log('No phone number selected', err);
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>
<h3>What about iOS?</h3>
<p>The bottom-sheet style is strictly an Android Play Services feature. For iOS, the native behavior actually <em>is</em> tied to the keyboard. On iOS devices, the code you originally wrote will work perfectly fine to show the number above the keyboard, provided the user has their phone number saved in their &quot;My Card&quot; in iOS Contacts:</p>
<pre><code>HTML
&lt;ion-input type=&quot;tel&quot; autocomplete=&quot;tel&quot;&gt;&lt;/ion-input&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>By combining your original HTML approach (for iOS) with the Capacitor plugin (for Android), you will get the exact native experience you are looking for on both platforms.</p>

Q: Calendar: adding a day when the button is clicked, in Java Swing A: <p>This is your answer:</p>
<pre><code>public class DateUtil
{
    public static Date addDays(Date date, int days)
    {
        Calendar cal = Calendar.getInstance();
        cal.setTime(date);
        cal.add(Calendar.DATE, days); //minus number would decrement the days
        return cal.getTime();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>To add one day, per click, call it like this:</p>
<pre><code>String sourceDate = &quot;2012-02-29&quot;;
SimpleDateFormat format = new SimpleDateFormat(&quot;yyyy-MM-dd&quot;);
Date myDate = format.parse(sourceDate);
myDate = DateUtil.addDays(myDate, 1);
</code></pre>
